From 085c86611da094d50da461ede0aa756a47efad9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhouyllll <xuezhou28@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:59:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 43/49] arch/arm64/rk3576: rule out bandwidth as the cause of
 CIF frame-tail truncation

Switching the write-to-DDR format from UNCOMPACT (16 bits per pixel,
3864 bytes per line) to COMPACT (packed RAW12, 2898 bytes) cuts the data
volume by 25%.  The truncation point did not move: still line 768 of 1096
at VMAX=1583, exactly as before.

That is a decisive negative.  It eliminates the whole family of
"insufficient throughput" explanations -- including the one I had been
pursuing.  The CIF advances at a fixed rate of roughly one written line
per 2.06 sensor line periods, independent of how many bytes each line
carries.  What has to be found is a rate divider, not a wider path.

Everything checked against the vendor driver so far matches bit for bit,
and is recorded here so it is not re-checked:

  CTRL0 = 0x92B  (CAPTURE | CROP | DMA | RAW12 parse (0x2<<1)<<3 |
                  UNCOMPACT (0x1<<5)<<3 | HIGH_ALIGN) -- identical
  CTRL1 = vc | dt<<2, HDR field zero for NO_HDR       -- identical
  SET_SIZE takes pixels (channel->width = pixm.width) -- identical
  VLW aligned to 256                                  -- identical
  N_LANES = lanes - 1                                 -- identical
  D-PHY 4 data lanes + clock lane                     -- identical
  CLK_VICAP_I3CLK gate = CLKGATE_CON(59) bit 4        -- identical
  aclk_vi_root / dclk_vicap both gpll/2 = 594MHz      -- already correct
  water-line flow control                             -- added, no effect

