From 32ff56afb16485af531d5db99f2455688796d211 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhouyllll <xuezhou28@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 13:37:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 48/49] arch/arm64/rk3576: the network port now works end to
 end

Two defects stood between a working link and working traffic, both of
them in how the interrupt reaches the CPU.

1. DMA_MODE.INTM routed the channel interrupt to a private line.

   The GMAC never asserted macirq.  Rather than keep guessing at the
   interrupt number, scan the GIC pending bitmap before and after
   traffic: a level interrupt nobody handles stays pending, so whichever
   bit lights up is the real line.  It was IRQ 336 (SPI 304) -- a number
   that appears nowhere in rk3576.dtsi -- while the dtsi gives gmac1
   macirq as SPI 301.  That is the signature of INTM being non-zero,
   which splits per-channel interrupts out of the common sbd_intr line.
   initdma() now clears INTM explicitly, and interrupts arrive on 333
   with NIS set, as they should.

2. The ISR did not clear the status bits.

   RK3576's GMAC interrupt is level triggered (IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH).
   The reference driver clears DMA_CH0_STATUS in the work queue, which
   is fine for a pulsed interrupt and fatal for a level one: the line
   stays asserted, the ISR re-enters immediately, and the board dies by
   watchdog.  Masking the DMA interrupt enable does not help either --
   the line is driven by the status bits, not by the enables.  The ISR
   now clears the status and hands the value to the worker through
   priv->dmasr, since the register it would otherwise read has already
   been cleared.  The DEBUGASSERT on work_available() became a plain
   check; with assertions on it was itself a way to crash.

Also corrected in passing: NIE/AIE are BIT(15)/BIT(14) here, not
BIT(16)/BIT(15).  Those names in dwmac4_dma.h belong to the original
4.00; every core from 4.10 on goes through dwmac410_enable_dma_irq(),
which uses the _4_10 pair.  Trying BIT(16) read back as zero -- the bit
is not implemented.  The readback is the evidence, not the macro name.

Measured on KICKPI-K7 against a switch: board to host 6/6 and host to
board 10/10, 0% loss, RTT around 0.5-2 ms at 1000M full duplex.

